{"parentUuid":"1a9e3fe6-08c0-4689-9ce0-d40ba58bbf60","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a0c5ef395232695eb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfY37ZJquaUwhhY2Wm7uK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CHY38tFoDzUbGfKxdHNTup","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-skills/64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82/scratchpad/r2/engine-skill-framework/ && cat gauntlet/PROTOCOL.md docs/05-critic-framework.md","description":"Read protocol and critic framework"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10065,"cache_read_input_tokens":41816,"output_tokens":175,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10065},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":175,"cache_read_input_tokens":41816,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10065,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10065,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01CHY38tFoDzUbGfKxdHNTup":{"command":"cd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-skills/64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82/scratchpad/r2/engine-skill-framework/ && cat gauntlet/PROTOCOL.md docs/05-critic-framework.md","description":"Read protocol and critic framework"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfY37Z3UGfgLfssncoaSS","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a9cc8d1a-75ae-4709-b9f3-0f5c1d506495","timestamp":"2026-09-29T16:21:34.923Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1"}
{"parentUuid":"a9cc8d1a-75ae-4709-b9f3-0f5c1d506495","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9bdfc07b-8ff1-4e95-9c88-de7440fd6192","agentId":"a0c5ef395232695eb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CHY38tFoDzUbGfKxdHNTup","type":"tool_result","content":"# Gauntlet G1 — Framework Gauntlet Protocol\n\nG1 answers one question: **do we have everything, with the right boundaries?** It does not judge whether any skill is *described* well; that is G2 (see `docs/08-phase-2-plan.md`).\n\n## The loop\n\n```\ndata/*.json ──render──▶ docs/01-05 (+ 00 rationale)\n      ▲                         │\n      │                  14 blind critics (fresh context each round)\n   revise                       │\n      │                  findings (schema below)\n      └──── dispositions ◀──────┘\n```\n\n1. **Gate.** `python3 scripts/check.py` must be green and `check.py --selftest` must go red on every mutation before a round starts. Critics never re-derive what the gate proves: unique owners, closure, layering, reference integrity.\n2. **Critique.** Every critic in `data/critics.json` (stage G1) reviews the whole framework in a fresh context. Critics receive the artifact only: `data/`, `docs/00-05`, and from round 2 on `docs/06-07`. They never receive this log, prior findings, or the builder's dispositions. That is the blind rule.\n3. **Findings.** Each finding has:\n   `id · critic · severity (blocker | major | minor) · type (omission | overlap | wrong-boundary | wrong-owner | obsolete-assumption | missing-contract | dependency-error | maturity-error | scale-down | other) · target (capability / skill / contract ids) · evidence · proposed change`.\n   - **blocker**: the framework cannot coordinate agents correctly (e.g. two owners, a missing domain that many skills need).\n   - **major**: a material omission or wrong boundary that would cause rework or silent legacy architecture.\n   - **minor**: a wording, naming or small-placement issue.\n   Stylistic preferences are not findings.\n4. **Disposition.** The builder assigns each finding one disposition: **accept** (change applied, commit referenced), **reject** (with a technical reason recorded), or **merge** (a duplicate of another finding). Dispositions are recorded in `gauntlet/round-N/dispositions.md`.\n5. **Revise, re-gate, re-render.** Every accepted change that is structural is also expressed in data, so the next gate checks it. A missing brief term becomes a `seed-map.json` entry.\n\n## Adjudication (added in round 1, K-QUALITY-4)\n\nThe builder does not get the last word on its own rejections. Every finding dispositioned **reject** or **partial** goes to a fresh-context **adjudicator**. The adjudicator receives the finding, the disposition text and the current data, but not the builder's other reasoning. It rules **uphold** (the disposition resolves the material problem) or **overturn** (the problem is still materially open). An overturned finding counts as an open accepted finding of its original severity and must be fixed in the next revision. Rulings are recorded in `gauntlet/round-N/adjudication.md`.\n\n## Critic calibration (added in round 1, K-QUALITY-4)\n\nFrom round 2 on, critics review a **seeded copy** of the framework in the scratchpad, not the repository. Before the round, the orchestrator plants 10–15 defects that `check.py` cannot see: omissions, overlaps, wrong owners, dishonest maturity labels, legacy stances and wrong platform tags. Each seed sits inside a specific critic's mandate. The seed list is sealed in `gauntlet/round-N/seeds.md` and is written only after the round.\n\n- A round **counts toward convergence only if the union of critics finds at least 80% of the seeds**, and each scoped critic finds the seeds in its own mandate at least once over two consecutive rounds.\n- Findings that hit a seed are removed before dispositions. The real framework never contained the seed.\n- A critic that misses its own seed twice in a row is re-briefed or replaced.\n\n## Convergence (stop rule)\n\nThe loop stops only when both hold:\n\n- **A.** In one full, *calibrated* round, all critics produce **zero accepted or adjudicator-overturned blocker or major findings**.\n- **B.** The **Completeness Critic** produces zero accepted material omissions in **two consecutive rounds**. Each round uses a fresh instance with no memory of the previous one.\n\nA re-raised finding that was rejected earlier counts as non-material unless it brings new evidence. A fixed round count is never a stop reason. If a round still has accepted blocker or major findings, another round runs.\n\n## Independence measures\n\n- Fresh context per critic per round; no shared scratchpad.\n- Critics do not see each other's output within a round.\n- The builder (lead agent) never grades its own fixes. The next round's critics do.\n- Critics are told the brief's standard: \"best practice\" means the strongest demonstrated approach today, not the most common historical one.\n<!-- GENERATED by scripts/render.py from data/*.json — do not edit by hand -->\n\n# 05 · Critic Framework\n\nCritics are independent reviewers with one mandate each. They run blind (fresh context, no author rationale, no prior-round history) and report only concrete, material deficiencies. See `gauntlet/PROTOCOL.md` for the loop, severity scale and convergence rule.\n\n## Stages\n\n- **G1** — Framework gauntlet: capability map, skill graph, contracts, hierarchy (whole framework)\n- **G2** — SKILL.md gauntlet: per-skill description quality, technique currency, validation criteria\n- **S1** — ADR review: evidence, alternatives, revisit conditions\n- **S2** — Design & contract review: API/contract drafts, data layouts, threading model\n- **S3** — Implementation review: code, tests, instrumentation\n- **S4** — Validation & release review: benchmarks, scalability runs, certification readiness\n\n## Critics\n\n### K-ARCH · Engine Architecture Critic\n\nArchitectural completeness and subsystem boundaries: ownership overlaps, missing contracts, wrong layering, contracts that leak internals, hierarchy that does not match coupling.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2\n- Scope: all, tier:orchestrator, research-evidence, subtree:api-lifecycle-migration, subtree:world-architect, developer-experience-docs\n- Checks: every capability has exactly one owner and the owner is the right expertise; non-responsibilities point to the real owner; contracts are minimal, stable and layered; no hidden cyclic build dependencies; leads have real territory, not just coordination\n\n### K-RENDER · Rendering Critic\n\nGraphics architecture and currency of rendering techniques; GPU-driven, bindless, explicit-API assumptions; scalability from 2D mobile to path tracing.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S3, S4\n- Scope: subtree:render-architect, render-validation, gpu-performance, terrain, vegetation-foliage, water-ocean, atmosphere-weather, deformation-skinning, xr-runtime, ml-inference-runtime, subtree:gpu-platform-architect, media-playback, voxel-worlds, platform-console\n- Checks: no draw-call-centric or DX11-era assumptions; render graph owns sync and aliasing; technique maturity labels are honest; scale-down path exists for each feature\n\n### K-SYSTEMS · Systems Critic\n\nConcurrency, memory, scheduling, IO and low-level architecture.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S3, S4\n- Scope: subtree:core-runtime-architect, subtree:resource-streaming-architect, observability-telemetry, crash-diagnostics, determinism-replay, hot-reload-iteration, rhi-core, gpu-memory-resources, render-graph-scheduling, audio-architect, network-transport, ml-inference-runtime, subtree:world-architect, subtree:gpu-platform-architect, runtime-scalability, media-playback\n- Checks: no single-thread-first assumptions; explicit ownership and lifetimes; synchronization minimized and justified; async IO end to end; heterogeneous cores and NUMA considered\n\n### K-SIM · Physics & Simulation Critic\n\nSimulation domains: physics, destruction, fluids, cloth, crowds, large-scale simulation, determinism of simulation.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S3, S4\n- Scope: subtree:physics-architect, crowd-simulation, ik-procedural-animation, prediction-rollback, determinism-replay, procedural-generation, water-ocean, vfx-particles, character-movement, simulation-validation, voxel-worlds\n- Checks: stepping and sync with frame phases are explicit; determinism levels are declared; simulation LOD exists for large scales; build-vs-integrate evidence\n\n### K-NET · Networking Critic\n\nOnline and multiplayer architecture: models, replication, prediction, servers, security, online-service boundaries.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S3, S4\n- Scope: subtree:network-architect, anti-cheat-integrity, platform-services, destruction-fracture, persistence-save, packaging-release-patching, character-movement, online-services-liveops, narrative-dialogue\n- Checks: netcode model matches game type; authority and trust explicit; bandwidth and scale budgets; version compatibility; service boundaries clear\n\n### K-TOOLS · Tools & Editor Critic\n\nProduction tooling and content workflows: editor, pipeline, collaboration, iteration speed.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S3, S4\n- Scope: subtree:editor-architect, subtree:content-pipeline-architect, hot-reload-iteration, developer-experience-docs, ui-architect, terrain, procedural-generation, cinematics-sequencer, scripting-runtime, world-data-model, subtree:build-release-architect, owns-area:TOOL, kind:tool\n- Checks: tooling as thorough as runtime; undo/transactions universal; large teams can collaborate without lock contention; iteration time measured; pipeline deterministic and cached\n\n### K-PLATFORM · Platform Critic\n\nHardware, operating systems, consoles, graphics APIs, packaging and platform requirements.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S4, S3\n- Scope: subtree:platform-architect, rhi-core, subtree:build-release-architect, certification-compliance, async-io-storage, crash-diagnostics, rhi-d3d12, rhi-vulkan, rhi-metal, rhi-webgpu\n- Checks: PAL is the only platform-specific layer; console, mobile and web constraints represented; certification requirements traced; packaging and patching per platform\n\n### K-PERF · Performance Critic\n\nScalability bottlenecks: serialization points, excess synchronization, cache inefficiency, GPU stalls, bandwidth, poor asymptotics.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S2, S3, S4\n- Scope: all, subtree:performance-architect, subtree:world-architect, runtime-scalability\n- Checks: budgets exist for each subsystem; benchmarks include realistic workloads; no hidden global sync points; asymptotics stated for world-scale data\n\n### K-PROD · Production Critic\n\nCould a real team ship games with this: workflows, content scale, team scale, schedules, live operations.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S4\n- Scope: all, tier:orchestrator, developer-experience-docs, subtree:build-release-architect, engine-product-management, reference-games\n- Checks: content creators are first-class; features have an owner through ship; live-ops and patching covered; scale-down keeps small teams productive\n\n### K-FUTURE · Future-Technology Critic\n\nDoes the architecture block likely next-5-year technologies: neural rendering, work graphs, cluster RT, ML animation, new storage and memory, new platforms.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1\n- Scope: all\n- Checks: extension points exist for emerging techniques; maturity labels allow promotion; no hard-coding of today's API limits\n\n### K-COMPLETE · Completeness Critic\n\nExactly one responsibility: find engine capabilities or engineering disciplines that are missing.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2\n- Scope: all\n- Checks: compare against shipped engines' feature sets; compare against platform requirements; compare against production pipelines end to end\n\n### K-LEGACY · Anti-Legacy Critic\n\nFind accidental legacy architecture: central sequential update loops, deep inheritance, global state, hot-path virtual dispatch, CPU-driven rendering, coarse locks, global renderer sync, frame-tied lifetimes, universal scene graphs, synchronous IO/loading, single-thread-first design, OpenGL/DX11 assumptions, monoliths. A flagged pattern needs justification, not removal.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S3, S4\n- Scope: all\n- Checks: each pattern in data/legacy-patterns.json absent or justified by an ADR\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY · Game Systems & Content Critic\n\nAnimation, audio, AI, gameplay framework, UI, text, localization and accessibility: whether game teams get what they need.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S4, S3\n- Scope: subtree:animation-architect, subtree:audio-architect, subtree:gameplay-architect, subtree:ui-architect, accessibility, input-system, input-devices-haptics, character-physics, cinematics-sequencer\n- Checks: designers and animators have authoring paths; accessibility and localization built in, not bolted on; gameplay extension points\n\n### K-TEST · Testing & Validation Critic\n\nValidation, oracles, determinism and concurrency testing, agent-output integrity: can an independent critic objectively decide correctness?\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S3, S4\n- Scope: all, subtree:test-architect, subtree:performance-architect\n- Checks: each skill has objective validation with an independent oracle; tests cannot be weakened silently; determinism & concurrency testable via seams; evidence bundle defined for S3/S4\n\n### K-SEC · Security Critic\n\nThreat model, trust boundaries, memory safety, supply chain, privacy, incident response — reviewed at design time as well as in code.\n\n- Stages: G1, G2, S1, S2, S3, S4\n- Scope: all, subtree:security-engineering, network-transport, scripting-runtime, modding-ugc, plugin-system, serialization-schema, package-formats-vfs, persistence-save, platform-services, online-services-liveops, packaging-release-patching, ci-cd-automation, build-system-toolchains, observability-telemetry, crash-diagnostics, editor-architect, ai-assisted-authoring, ai-behavior-perception, dedicated-server, asset-import-interchange\n- Checks: every untrusted input has a validating owner and a fuzz target; trust boundaries explicit; hardening & key custody; privacy of telemetry, crash and player data\n\n## Coverage matrix (stage G2 — which critics review which SKILL.md)\n\n| Skill | ARCH | RENDER | SYSTEMS | SIM | NET | TOOLS | PLATFORM | PERF | PROD | FUTURE | COMPLETE | LEGACY | GAMEPLAY | TEST | SEC |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| engine-architect | ◆ |  |  |  |  |  |  | · | ◆ | · | · | · |  | · | · |\n| architecture-governance | ◆ |  |  |  |  |  |  | · | ◆ | · | · | · |  | · | · |\n| program-orchestration | ◆ |  |  |  |  |  |  | · | ◆ | · | · | · |  | · | · |\n| research-evidence | ◆ |  |  |  |  |  |  | · | · | · | · | · |  | · | · |\n| performance-architect | · |  |  |  |  |  |  | ◆ | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| perf-benchmarking | · |  |  |  |  |  |  | ◆ | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| cpu-performance | · |  |  |  |  |  |  | ◆ | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| gpu-performance | · | ◆ |  |  |  |  |  | ◆ | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| loading-streaming-performance | · |  |  |  |  |  |  | ◆ | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| test-architect | · |  |  |  |  |  |  | · | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| render-validation | · | ◆ |  |  |  |  |  | · | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| functional-automation-soak | · |  |  |  |  |  |  | · | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| robustness-fuzzing | · |  |  |  |  |  |  | · | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| certification-compliance | · |  |  |  |  |  | ◆ | · | · | · | · | · |  | ◆ | · |\n| security-engineering | · |  |  |  |  |  |  | · | · | · | · | · |  | · | ◆ |\n| anti-cheat-integrity | · |  |  |  | ◆ |  |  | · | · | · | · | · |  | · | ◆ |\n| observability-telemetry | · |  | ◆ |  |  |  |  | · | · | · | · | · |  | · | ◆ |\n| crash-diagnostics | · |  | ◆ |  |  |  | ◆ | · | · | · | · | · |  | · | ◆ |\n| determinism-replay | · |  | ◆ | ◆ |  |  |  | · | · | · | · | · |  | · | · |\n